# Runtime caches that must never be committed. xcproxy writes a ~20MB
# tmdb_cache.db into its resources/ dir; equestria-cluster ignores these
# and home-operations did not, so a verbatim copy during the piece 21
# migration committed it. Matches equestria-cluster's own rule.
*.db*
# Crew: ignore runtime state (logs, inbox, sessions)
# Note: .crew/casting/* is identity and MUST be committed to main, not ignored
.crew/orchestration-log/
.crew/log/
.crew/decisions/inbox/
.crew/sessions/
.crew/.scratch/
.crew/.cache/
# Crew: state owned by crew-state branch (two-layer/orphan backend)
.crew/decisions.md
.crew/agents/*/history.md
# /Crew: state owned by crew-state branch
# Crew: SubCrew activation file (local to this machine)
.crew-workstream

# Pkl VS Code extension VSIX cache (installed via the mise `pkl-vscode` task)
.pkl-vscode/

# Talos / Kubernetes client credentials.
#
# `talosconfig` carries an unencrypted `os:admin` client certificate and its
# private key — full machine-API control: config read/write, reboot, reset,
# and machine secrets. It must never be committed. equestria-cluster ignored
# it at its repo root; the talhelper migration brought over
# talos/clusterconfig/.gitignore, whose rule only scopes to that subdirectory,
# so talos/talosconfig one level up was left uncovered and got committed.
# Bare patterns here, so they match at any depth.
kubeconfig
talosconfig

# ...with one exception: the mise task is named after the file it fetches, and
# "any depth" includes .config/mise/tasks/talos/. Without this line `git add`
# silently skips the task that CREATES the ignored kubeconfig — no error, it
# just never appears in the commit.
!.config/mise/tasks/talos/kubeconfig
